1. e4 e5 2. Bc4 Nf6 3. d4 Nc6 4. Bxf7+ Kxf7 5. dxe5 Nxe5 6. f4 Nc6 7. e5 d5 8.
exf6 Qxf6 9. Nf3 Bb4+ 10. c3 Re8+ 11. Kf1 Bd6 12. Ng5+ Kg6 13. Qd3+ Qf5 14. Qg3
Qg4 15. Qxg4 Bxg4 16. h3 Be2+ 17. Kf2 Bc5+ 18. Kg3 Ne7 19. Kh2 Nf5 20. g4 Ne3
21. Na3 Bd6 22. Kg3 h6 23. Nf3 Re4 24. Kf2 Bxf3 25. Kxf3 Bxf4 26. Bxe3 Rxe3+
27. Kf2 Rae8 28. Rad1 Re2+ 29. Kf3 Bg5 30. Rd3 Bh4 31. Rf1 {"SIR G. THOMAS’S
ERROR Thomas made an even odder mistake against Milner-Barry. He sacrificed a
piece on the fourth move in the Bishop’s opening, under the impression that
he would recover it by a check with his Queen on Q 5. Unfortunately the square
was protected by a black knight. How­ever, it was a game after all, for
Milner-Barry, though he might perhaps have managed to hold on to the piece,
preferred to give it back imme­diately in order to give himself a long start
in development. He thereby obtained a strong attack, and though Thomas
wriggled in­geniously in a most unprepossessing position, Milner-Barry
eventually broke through with his rooks. After this the White king was in a
mating net, and though Black missed a forced mate by the sacrifice of a bishop,
his own way was good enough to win." (The Times, 3 January 1939)} 0-1

